---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
---

<CaseLayout
  headline="Designing a B2B buying experience where sales partners and account teams could finish the deal together."
  intro={[
    'Hitachi Energy’s sales partners buy industrial equipment on behalf of end customers. They could already find products online, but configuration and quotation still spilled into email, spreadsheets, and calls.',
    'I led the service and interaction design with two other designers over six months. We connected the partner storefront to the account teams behind each quote, giving both sides one place to work from discovery through agreement.',
  ]}
  stakeholder="Hitachi Energy"
  skills={['Discovery', 'User Experience Design', 'Journey Mapping']}
  year="2021-22 (6 months)"
  role="Senior Designer"
  teamLabel="Team of 3 designers"
  hero="/assets/hitachi/video/partner-commerce-overview.webm"
  heroVideo
  heroPoster="/assets/hitachi/posters/partner-commerce-overview.jpg"
  heroCaption="Senior Designer · Six months. I connected product discovery and item-level quotation to the account-team conversation."
  theme="hitachi"
  previousHref="/work/global-data-analytics/"
  nextHref="/work/cisco-customer-insights/"
>
  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Context</h2>
    <p>A partner might buy dozens of configurable products for a customer tender. Price depended on the order, delivery terms, compatible components, and what a factory could fulfill.</p>
    <p>That made the transaction a collaboration between two people: a sales partner assembling the order and a Hitachi account manager coordinating factories, engineers, and commercial terms.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hitachi/partner-account-team.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1685}
    alt="Sales partner and Hitachi account manager with their responsibilities"
    caption="The two sides of the experience: a sales partner purchasing for an end customer, and a Hitachi account manager coordinating the people and conditions needed to fulfill it."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Mapping the Partner Journey</h2>
    <p>I led working sessions with business partners in Germany, India, Thailand, and Australia while learning the configuration, pricing, and quotation process.</p>
    <p>The journey map compared what partners were trying to accomplish with the touchpoints, constraints, and account-team activity behind each step. It exposed a sharp break: the storefront covered browsing and configuration, then disappeared as soon as the relationship work began.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hitachi/journey-map.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1687}
    alt="Synthesized journey from product browsing through order completion"
    caption="The partner journey put browsing, configuration, quotation, and negotiation in one view, including the constraints and off-platform work in each phase."
  />

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hitachi/platform-space-map.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1687}
    alt="Map of platform spaces and activities"
    caption="I translated the journey into digital spaces and flows so product and engineering could see where each activity belonged and where the quotation loop needed to reconnect."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Defining the Quotation Experience</h2>
    <p>The roadmap proposed a quote button and an open text field. A partner could begin a request online, but every meaningful exchange after that would still happen elsewhere.</p>
    <p>A partial solution risked making the process harder to follow: the platform would show that a quote had started without showing what had changed, who was waiting, or whether the order was ready to place.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hitachi/original-quotation-boundary.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1684}
    alt="Original flow ending at proposal generation"
    caption="The original plan digitized the trigger, then handed quotation and negotiation back to offline channels."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Designing the Quotation Conversation</h2>
    <p>I broke real quotation exchanges into two kinds of input. Partners usually opened with a structured request such as a discount, delivery date, commerce term, or product-specific change. The response was less predictable. It might include a counteroffer, explanation, attachment, or exception.</p>
    <p><strong>Comparable requests needed structure. Negotiation needed room for a conversation.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hitachi/quotation-conversation-model.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1689}
    alt="Quotation conversations translated into structured and flexible interaction patterns"
    caption="Conversation analysis separated a quote’s structured initiation from the flexible negotiation that followed, giving each a different interaction model."
  />

  <div class="media-grid quotation-concepts" data-reveal>
    <Figure
      src="/assets/hitachi/video/quotation-concept-inline.webm"
      poster="/assets/hitachi/posters/quotation-concept-inline.jpg"
      width={1436}
      height={1022}
      alt="Inline item-level quote adjustment prototype"
      caption="Concept 01: item-level adjustment requests stayed close to the product rows they changed."
      video
      controls
    />
    <Figure
      src="/assets/hitachi/video/quotation-concept-panel.webm"
      poster="/assets/hitachi/posters/quotation-concept-panel.jpg"
      width={1436}
      height={1022}
      alt="Quote adjustment side-panel prototype"
      caption="Concept 02: a persistent panel made it easier to move between several adjusted items in a large order."
      video
      controls
    />
  </div>

  <section class="case-section reading-column case-transition" data-reveal>
    <h2>Prototyping and Roadmap Planning</h2>
    <p>The prototypes showed that shipping only the request form would preserve the email handoff. I used them in roadmap reviews to argue for delaying release and bringing quotation into the platform.</p>
    <p>Product, engineering, and business leadership agreed to change the scope. I then cut batch actions and product recommendations from the first release. That kept item-level quotation intact without adding another round of delay.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hitachi/updated-quotation-flow.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1685}
    alt="Updated end-to-end quotation flow"
    caption="The revised flow kept quote creation, account-team review, partner negotiation, approval, and order placement connected inside the product."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Designing Item-Level Negotiation</h2>
    <p>Bulk orders made a single quote-level form too blunt. A partner might request a delivery change on one item, a discount on another, and accept the remaining products as quoted.</p>
    <p>I designed a flexible product tile that could hold configurations, add-ons, lead time, requested changes, and account-team responses without sending people through a stack of panels.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hitachi/flexible-product-tile.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1008}
    alt="Product tile across cart, quote request, and updated quote states"
    caption="The product tile became the shared unit across cart review, request creation, and the account team’s revised quote."
  />

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hitachi/video/itemized-quotation-flow.webm"
    poster="/assets/hitachi/posters/itemized-quotation-flow.jpg"
    width={1920}
    height={1080}
    alt="Itemized quotation workflow"
    caption="The itemized flow let partners apply requests to individual products while retaining a readable view of the full order."
    video
    controls
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column case-transition" data-reveal>
    <h2>Designing Configuration Dependencies</h2>
    <p>The same component model also had to carry configuration dependencies. When one selection required another component, the interface explained the relationship in context and blocked an invalid cart state.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hitachi/video/configuration-error-handling.webm"
    poster="/assets/hitachi/posters/configuration-error-handling.jpg"
    width={1920}
    height={1080}
    alt="Product configuration dependency and error handling"
    caption="Configuration feedback showed the required add-on beside the affected selection, keeping recovery inside the product-building flow."
    video
    controls
    contain
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Impact</h2>
    <p>Nine to twelve months after launch, the business team shared follow-up feedback from partners and account teams: deals were reaching completion about three weeks sooner than on the previous platform.</p>
    <p>They also reported fewer periods when active opportunities went quiet. Our engagement had ended, so I could not check the estimate against transaction data and treat it as directional. The explanation was consistent: both sides now had a current record of the decisions holding up the deal.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>What I learned</h2>
    <p><strong>The useful boundary was between comparable information and open-ended negotiation.</strong></p>
    <p>We could structure requests that followed known patterns. We still needed to give partners and account managers room to work through the exceptions together.</p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
